# GH #38 shadowing is not applied when a workspace type collides with a typedb funcdef

## Steps to Reproduce

1. TODO: Add steps

## Expected Behavior

TODO: Describe expected behavior

## Actual Behavior

TODO: Describe actual behavior

Found 2026-08-31 by the B060 lane while fixing a regression of its own. It is
NOT that regression — this one is on master and on the branch alike, resolves
upstream of any code B060 touched, and was deliberately left alone there.

## Defect

A LOCAL declared with a workspace type whose name collides with a typedb
funcdef resolves to the ENGINE funcdef, not to the workspace declaration:

```angelscript
class CoroutineFunc {}          // the plugin's own type
void f() {
    CoroutineFunc@ local;       // errors: resolved to the engine funcdef
}
```

GH #38 establishes that a plugin's own declaration shadows an engine one. That
rule is applied for class-vs-class collisions. It is NOT applied when the
colliding engine symbol is a **funcdef**, which is a different code path.

## Where

Upstream in type resolution, before the checker's argument-shape logic runs.
B060's `GlobalScope::workspace_shadows_funcdef` fixes the ARGUMENT-position
half of this family (a value of such a type reaching a call slot); the local
declaration path is untouched and is what this ticket is about.

## Severity

A false positive — the LSP rejects a declaration the game accepts — which is
the damaging direction. Bounded in practice: it needs a plugin to name a type
exactly after a Core funcdef. No fixture does, which is precisely why no gate
in this batch could see the related regressions (see below).

## Test plan (TDD, red first)

Red: `class CoroutineFunc {}` plus `CoroutineFunc@ local;` in a function.

Variants, per repo CLAUDE.md — B060 established these axes matter, so sweep
them rather than pinning one:

- each shadowing SHAPE: class, interface, enum, `typedef` (note a typedef
  reuses `SymbolKind::Funcdef` and `lookup_funcdef` deliberately skips it, then
  falls through to the typedb — that is the subtle hole)
- each POSITION: local, parameter, return type, member field, `array<T>` element
- the NAMESPACE axis: a type declared inside `namespace Nz` arrives at the
  checker as a BARE name, because a value's type text is stored as written.
  Any exact-name guard misses this no matter how many shapes it enumerates.
- a NON-COLLIDING control of each shape, so the test distinguishes "shadowing
  works" from "this shape is simply never treated as a funcdef"
- a real engine funcdef with NO workspace collision must still resolve to the
  engine funcdef

## Related

GH #38 (class-vs-class shadowing, the rule this path does not honour), B060
(fixed the argument-position half; `workspace_shadows_funcdef` is the predicate
to reuse or generalize).

## Three reproductions from lane-b060-b062 (2026-08-31)

All three share one cause: **GH #38 shadowing is not applied when a workspace
type collides with a typedb funcdef**, resolved upstream in type resolution
before B060's code runs. Each was verified identical on the merge-base and the
B060 branch binaries, so all three are **pre-existing on master** and none is
a B060 regression.

1. **Local variable.** `class CoroutineFunc { int v; }` then
   `CoroutineFunc@ c = CoroutineFunc();` - `Text::Format("%d", c)` errors on
   both binaries, and the constructor call reports
   `function CoroutineFunc expects 1 args, got 0`. The declared type resolves
   to the engine funcdef, so the CTOR resolves to the funcdef ctor too.
2. **Funcdef constructor path.** `try_funcdef_ctor` (`checker.rs`) calls
   `lookup_funcdef` directly, and `call_type` tries it BEFORE the workspace
   type constructor, so
   `class CoroutineFunc {}; CoroutineFunc@ v; auto x = CoroutineFunc(v);`
   is classified as Core funcdef construction rather than the local class.
   Silent on both binaries - a MISSED diagnostic rather than a false one.
3. **Ctor-call spelling** as in (1) - same root, different surface.

## Reuse the predicate B060 already built

The argument-position half of this is already fixed on the B060 branch by
`GlobalScope::workspace_shadows_funcdef`, and whoever takes B073 should reuse
it rather than reinvent it. The rule, stated once:

> **Only a TYPE declaration shadows a TYPE name.**

- Types: class / interface / enum / typedef. **Typedef is included
  deliberately** because it reuses `SymbolKind::Funcdef` and `lookup_funcdef`
  skips it.
- A variable or function sharing the name is a VALUE and shadows nothing.
- Applied over the enclosing-namespace ladder and the tail table, resolving
  **exactly one** candidate in lexical order.

That predicate cost four review rounds to get right on B060, in both
directions: an early version UNDER-blocked (a list of shapes that missed enum,
typedef and namespace-relative names) and a later one OVER-blocked (accepting
any workspace symbol, so an unrelated namespace-level variable stood in for a
type and suppressed real errors). Both edges are now pinned by tests. Do not
re-derive it from scratch.

## Sequencing

B073 should be taken AFTER B060 merges, so the predicate is available in
master rather than needing to be copied off a branch.

## Located (coordinator, 2026-08-31) — the ticket's "upstream in type resolution" is `resolver.rs`

Measured, not inferred. The path that lets an engine funcdef win over a
workspace declaration of the same name is:

    src/typecheck/resolver.rs:139   if self.scope.lookup_funcdef(&qualified).is_some()
    src/typecheck/resolver.rs:177   if self.scope.lookup_funcdef(&candidate).is_some()
    src/typecheck/resolver.rs:191   if self.scope.lookup_funcdef(&resolved).is_some()
    src/typecheck/resolver.rs:197   if self.scope.lookup_funcdef(&resolved).is_some()

All four ask `lookup_funcdef` directly. That helper walks workspace symbols but
only RETURNS on a real funcdef, then falls through to the typedb — so a
workspace class, interface, enum or typedef named after a Core funcdef resolves
to the ENGINE funcdef. That is the same fall-through B060 documented; B060
fixed only the argument-position half, in `checker.rs`, via
`GlobalScope::workspace_shadows_funcdef`.

**The fix is very likely to reuse `workspace_shadows_funcdef` at these four
sites** rather than to introduce a new predicate — but read B079 first. That
ticket's whole subject is callers reaching for the wrong member of a set of
near-identical predicates, and adding a fifth caller of one is exactly the
move that needs a moment's thought rather than a reflex.

## Sequencing

**Blocked on lane-b057's B079 Move 2, not on the checker lane.** I previously
recorded this as a `checker.rs` conflict; that was assumed rather than
measured, and it is wrong. B073 is a `resolver.rs` change, and Move 2 touches
`resolver.rs:165` — adjacent to this ticket's 139-197. B071 and B076 are the
`checker.rs` ones.

## Still valid from the original filing

Not fixed by B060, and the ticket says so explicitly: B060's
`workspace_shadows_funcdef` covers a VALUE of such a type reaching a call slot;
the LOCAL DECLARATION path (`CoroutineFunc@ local;`) is untouched. Verified
still present.

Severity unchanged: a false positive, the damaging direction — the LSP rejects
a declaration the game accepts. Bounded in practice because it needs a plugin
to name a type exactly after a Core funcdef, and no fixture does, **which is
precisely why no gate in this batch could see the related regressions.** That
last point is the reason this is worth fixing rather than shrugging at: the
absence of a fixture is not evidence of absence in the wild, it is the reason
the wild goes unmeasured.

## FIXED (lane-resolver, 2026-08-31) — commit e3e1241 on lane/resolver

`SUITE-VERIFIED: 2397 passed; 0 failed; 14 ignored; 26/26 binaries reported`
(2388 + 9). Merged by coordinator; see the merge commit for final state.

### Change — 2 production hunks and one predicate composition

- **`src/typecheck/resolver.rs:136`** — `resolve_named` establishes
  `workspace_shadowing_type_name(&qualified)` BEFORE the `lookup_funcdef` and
  `is_angelscript_builtin_type` shortcuts (both of which answer from the
  ENGINE), and lets it win at `:179` — after the primitive/void checks, so a
  keyword-spelled type still cannot be shadowed.
- **`src/typecheck/checker.rs:2530`** — `try_funcdef_ctor` declines when the
  workspace shadows, handing the call to the type-constructor path below.
- **`src/typecheck/global_scope.rs:1602`** — new
  `workspace_shadowing_type_name -> Option<String>`;
  `workspace_shadows_funcdef` becomes `.is_some()` of it. **Composition, not
  addition** (the B079 convention): zero behavioural change to its five
  existing callers. `any_workspace_symbol` likewise becomes `.is_some()` of a
  new private `first_workspace_symbol`.

The fix returns the **declaration's qualified name, not the spelling**. That is
what makes the namespaced case produce
`UndefinedMember { object_type: "Nz::CoroutineFunc" }` matching its control
exactly, and it is the detail most likely to be "simplified" back later — the
namespace test is the only thing that would catch that.

### THE HOLE WAS IN TWO PATHS, NOT ONE

`awaitable` is **not** a typedb funcdef — it is hardcoded in
`is_angelscript_builtin_type`, and that check had the identical hole. Guarding
`lookup_funcdef` alone leaves those four cells red.

Same lesson B071 and B076 carry from the other direction: a check has a natural
home, and the natural home is one of N sites. Here the ticket implied N=1 and
the answer was N=2. It was found by testing the BEHAVIOUR rather than the
function. Kept as a separate test for that reason.

### Deliberately NOT guarded — do not "complete" this later without reading

The ticket's "Located" note points at all four `lookup_funcdef` sites in
resolver.rs. **Only one was guarded.** The other three are reached only after a
name has FAILED to resolve, which cannot happen while the workspace declares a
type of that name — an exact declaration answers at the first site, and a
namespaced one answers there too through the tail table.

Two further sites in checker.rs were probed and left alone for the same reason:
`funcdef_name_of` (2540) and `slot_funcdef` (2602). Both produce output
identical to the non-colliding control today.

**Three dead guards would have read as protection.** A guard that cannot fire
is indistinguishable at review from one that can, so it makes the next reader
believe an axis is covered when nothing is checking it — the mirror image of
B076, where a real check reached one of four positions and LOOKED like it
reached all four.

The substitute is a tripwire, not an argument:
`a_plugin_type_named_after_an_engine_funcdef_behaves_the_same_at_a_call_slot`
goes red if either checker site ever becomes reachable-and-shadowing, forcing a
deliberate decision instead of a discovery.

### Red-then-green (production hunks reverse-applied with tests in place)

6 red / 3 green before, 9/9 after.

- **24 resolver cells red**: {CoroutineFunc, CoroutineFuncUserdataString} x
  {class, interface, enum, typedef} x {top-level bare, namespaced bare from
  inside, namespaced bare from outside} — all gave `Funcdef("CoroutineFunc")`,
  want `Named(...)`. The 4th cell of each row (namespaced, spelled fully
  qualified) already worked and is an **in-sweep control**.
- **4 builtin-table cells red** — the `awaitable` path above.
- **5 checker position rows red**: local, parameter, member field,
  `array<T>` element, `T@[]` element all LOST their `UndefinedMember`
  (return-type position already worked). Each paired against a non-colliding
  `ZZZWidget` control of the same shape in the same position and required to
  agree, with an anti-vacuity floor on how many control rows diagnosed anything.
- **ctor red**: `CoroutineFunc()` on the plugin's own class gave
  `ArgCountMismatch expects 1, got 0` — the false positive, and the damaging
  direction.
- **namespace qualifier red**, **call-slot parity red**.

**Three pure controls, green in BOTH directions** (the B060 over-blocking
edge): a workspace VARIABLE or FUNCTION of the name shadows nothing and the
engine funcdef still wins; a non-colliding type of each shape still resolves to
itself; an unshadowed engine funcdef still constructs, still binds a matching
function, and still rejects a mismatched one.

No test expectation was changed.